[
  {
    "id": "example-room-lighting-room-lighting-example",
    "type": "comment",
    "name": "Room lighting example",
    "info": "Needs: A Zigbee2MQTT light on your broker. Injects stand in for the sensors and wall switch.\n\nMotion and illuminance feed occupancy fusion and the motion policy; a wall switch feeds the switch policy. Both send lighting intents to the room controller, which owns the light's commands. The circadian lighting policy sends the room how its lights should look over the day; the room uses it for lights nobody has set otherwise.\n\nReplace the inject nodes with your sensors. The room reaches its light through the zigbee2mqtt light node: set its friendly name. Set the circadian policy's wake and bed times (Node-RED host time) before deploying. To see the room in Home Assistant, give the room controller an area and choose what it publishes (each light's control state, and its discovery); the same for the circadian policy's circadian and sleep switches. They control and style the lights either way.\n\nBefore deploying, open the \"your MQTT broker\" config node (any node's Broker setting) and set your broker's host: every node here that talks MQTT subscribes and publishes through it, with no MQTT in, MQTT out or status nodes.",
    "x": 170,
    "y": 40,
    "wires": []
  },
  {
    "id": "example-room-lighting-motion-detected",
    "type": "inject",
    "name": "motion detected",
    "props": [
      {
        "p": "payload",
        "v": "{\"motion\":true}",
        "vt": "json"
      },
      {
        "p": "source",
        "v": "motion",
        "vt": "str"
      }
    ],
    "repeat": "",
    "crontab": "",
    "once": false,
    "onceDelay": 0.1,
    "topic": "",
    "x": 150,
    "y": 100,
    "wires": [
